WEBVTT

00:00:00.138 --> 00:00:03.267
Adding more grouping columns makes the summary more detailed.

00:00:03.267 --> 00:00:06.437
Here we group by patient and diagnosis together.

00:00:06.437 --> 00:00:10.922
One output row now represents one patient-and-diagnosis combination.

00:00:10.922 --> 00:00:15.642
Run the example and compare it with the previous one-row-per-diagnosis query.

00:00:15.642 --> 00:00:18.732
You should see more rows because the grain is more specific.

00:00:18.732 --> 00:00:20.367
This is an important design choice.

00:00:20.367 --> 00:00:23.817
If the question asks how many admissions each diagnosis has overall,

00:00:23.817 --> 00:00:26.242
patient ID does not belong in the grouping.

00:00:26.242 --> 00:00:32.627
If the question asks how many admissions each patient has for each diagnosis, then both columns are required.

00:00:32.627 --> 00:00:39.262
When a grouped query returns too many or too few rows, inspect the GROUP BY list first and restate the

00:00:39.262 --> 00:00:42.812
intended grain in plain language.

00:00:42.812 --> 00:00:44.602
GROUP BY changes the grain of the result.

00:00:44.602 --> 00:00:50.072
If you group by department and month, one output row represents one department-month combination.

00:00:50.072 --> 00:00:52.147
State that sentence before adding measures.

00:00:52.147 --> 00:00:58.172
It becomes a quick check for whether every selected non-aggregate column belongs in the grouping.
